n the introduction of the virus  into the human population  but the more pressing question  identifying the intermediary species  that might have transmitted the virus  from bats to humans  remains a work in progress  joining me now one of the members of the  who investigation team epidemiologist dr  fabian linda is from the prestigious  robert koch institute here in berlin  and was assisting the team in china from  right here  dr linda it's a pleasure to have you on  the program now am i correct in  understanding that the team isn't sure  if the coronavirus originated in china  we still have no good evidence for the  place where the spillover has happened  that's right  do you think the team would have had a  better idea  if it had sort of been in china much  earlier  well you know it's it's always the  earlier the better because  the tracks are still fresher however  it's not  too late many of the investigations on  zoonotic diseases  have started much much later than this  one so i think there's still a good  chance  to find out what happened so you are  convinced that we that there will come a  time in our future that we will know  where the coronavirus actually  originated from  this is a very complicated puzzle  especially since this virus causes often  mild  symptoms or no symptoms so it's harder  to follow back the tracks  to the patient zero the index case  however i'm very confident that at some  point we will know  which spat species harbors the right  virus  or the ancestor of all these human  viruses and perhaps we get an idea about  intermediate host which may have played  a role  in the transmission if we will ever find  out  where exactly and how exactly it  happened that's a big question mark  the team spent about one month in china  of which two weeks was spent  in quarantine indoors do you think this  was  sufficient time to try and investigate  the origins of the coronavirus  so the the whole mission is taking much  much longer than that we had discussions  with our chinese colleagues  in the weeks before and the mission  still continues even if the team is not  on site  so um that was the maximum we could  free our time for now and the the  the aim of this first mission was anyway  just to see  what the chinese colleagues have done  already which data are there and where  are the gaps to design the future  studies  so i think the time frame was okay for  that do you think the data that was  shared with the whole team from the  chinese side  uh was sufficient and did you find that  the chinese side were  transparent and helpful in their  dealings  so that the data are not sufficient but  not because  they were hiding them from us it's  because they're not  there yet so this these are these future  study which have to start now  and which will hopefully uh fill the  gaps in that big  puzzle um our impression was  that um we did not get  any evidence for them hiding concrete  data from us we got to saw some of the  raw data  we discussed all the figures and we  really had in-depth  investigations together so the general  gut feeling is a good one  however as everybody knows who has  children you never know what they're  hiding  uh the team also said in the press  conference that  there there could be a possibility of  so-called cold chain transmission  of the virus which is to say that the  virus could have spread  through frozen food packaging for  instance now this is a theory that china  has been putting forward  especially in the past few months would  you say therefore that china has been  vindicated  well so in the in the recent outbreaks  in in china they have  generated good evidence that those  outbreaks  had something to do with the cold chain  and cold chain products  however those are now new outbreaks  starting in the middle of a pandemic  where there are  lots of people around the world who can  contaminate  food packages so the question if that  played a role also at the beginning of  the pandemic is something  we need many more data on and the food  package is nothing where  the virus is born right it's not coming  from a food package that food package  has to be  contaminated either by a symptomatic  human  or by an animal which carries the virus  and so  that will be really difficult to  pinpoint so a little bit too much  attention on the food package  because it's just a vehicle right right  now a lot has been made about the  origins of the coronavirus and there is  of course been a lot of  politics and finger-pointing over it but  just scientifically speaking  do you think it's even important to know  where the coronavirus originated from  i think the most important thing is to  understand how  it emerged because only if we understand  a problem we can  try to take counter measures in the  future to reduce the risk of something  like that  happening again if this event happened  10 meters to the left or to the right to  a border between two countries  really doesn't matter dr fabian  linda thank you so much for putting it  into perspective we leave it there for  the time being thank you so much for  speaking to us  thank you
 

